Patric Mueller wrote:
> Kent Paul Dolan <xanthian@well.com> wrote:
>> Patric Mueller wrote:
...
>>> I guess that there are players who would oppose a
>>> NetHack successor that would not be a fork from
>>> the current code base.
>> Why on earth? _Anything_ that worked as NetHack
>> works, but was in some form of maintainable code,
>> would be a huge win for the whole community, both in
>> terms of likelihood of being low on bugs, and in
>> terms of allowing exploration of game additions by
>> user-written patches.
...
> One reason, although not a rational one, why NeoHack could be
> shunned by some players is exactly the one that JG Miller is always
> propagating.
> 
> It wouldn't be NetHack or a NetHack variant/fork as it would break the
> code lineage from the first version of Hack. NeoHack would be a clone
> or a rewrite of NetHack in the same way that Hack was "only" a clone
> and not a variant of Rogue.
...

That's not a rational reason for "shunning" a new roguelike, but it _is_ 
a rational reason for not seeing one as a replacement for NetHack itself 
(and I'm happy to see folks using "NeoHack" as a designation for the 
current discussion rather than continuing Miller's claim that such a 
project should try to take over NetHack's name).

I'm happy to see any new roguelike games developed, regardless of how 
much narrative or mechanics they borrow from pre-existing games, but I 
think it'd be reasonable to object to (and even shun) a new project that 
tried to take on the name of a pre-existing game.

The /dev/null/nethack Tournament is a _NetHack_ tournament and will 
always be run on vanilla NetHack; much as we may like to add content 
around the vanilla core, that material will always be secondary to the 
central tournament.  We'd be happy to have other roguelike's involved 
(like we did with ZAPM this year, or as adjuncts to the main tournament 
in some other way) or to help folks set up new tournaments for other 
roguelikes, and if/when NeoHack is ready we'd be just as happy to 
involve it this way as well.

I only have two problems with Miller: he wants to take over the name 
"NetHack" and he seems to want to do it without doing any actual work. 
Other than those two issues, I don't think there's any problem with his 
complaints (in that they are mostly subjective issues that affect his 
enjoyment of the game and likely affect many others' the same way) or to 
the proposal to start a new project to address them.

-robin


